{
  "transcript": "Good morning, Mr. Sharma. Please take a seat. What primary symptoms bring you to the eye clinic today? Good morning, doctor. Over the past month, I have noticed progressive metamorphosis in my blood pressure in my left eye, along with persistent asthenopia and photophobia. Yesterday evening, I saw a transient photopsia and my eyes are floating back.\nGiven the sudden onset of photopsia and vitreous floaters, we must evaluate for posterior vitreous detachment or a peripheral retinal tear. First, we will check the visual acuity, then perform dynamic retinoscopy and Goldman Appalachian tonometry to measure your intracular pressure and screen.\nUnder slit-lamp bio-microscopy, your anterior segment, including the cornea and iridocorneal angle, is unremarkable.\nEpithelium atrophy, drusen deposition, and mild localized macular edema. Is it age-related macular degeneration, doctor, or diabetic rectaloscopy? It is dry age-related muscular degeneration. Spectral domain optical coherence tomography shows disruption of the ellipsoid zone.\nIn addition, there is a short window diffraction without microneumatism or cotton wool spots. What is the prognosis and what they suggest?\nTherapies are available. Since there is no activate neovascular membrane, anti-VEGF intraviral injection, B-Rani-Bilumab are not indicated. We will initiate oral antioxidant, R-Cyte-H2 supplement to deter disease progression and give you an amstrad for home organ. If white conversion occurs, another dynamic therapy will be considered.\nUnder slit-lamp bio-microscopy, drusen deposition, and mild localized macular edema. Is it age-related macular degeneration, drusen deposition, and mild localized macular degeneration, B-Rani-Bilumab are not indicated. We will initiate oral antioxidant, R-Cyte-H2 supplement to deter disease progression and give you an amstrad for home organ. If white conversion occurs, another dynamic therapy will be considered.",
